Cataracts
Geriatric & Frailty
Gradual painless blurred vision, Increased glare sensitivity/halos, Yellowish tint to vision, Visible opacification of the lens

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-004
Reason for Visit: Acute distress following a dangerous near-miss fall secondary to severe visual decompensation in low light.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated the patient suffered a severe near-miss fall on the stairs tonight due to acute disorientation caused by extreme glare sensitivity and visual blurring. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to evaluate for occult traumatic injury, mitigate immediate environmental hazards, and assess visual acuity safety thresholds prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I completely lost my footing on the stairs because the lights were blinding me, and everything went blurry and yellow; I'm terrified to move around my own house."
History of Present Illness: Over the past two years, the patient reports gradual, progressive, and completely painless blurring of vision. Recently, there has been significantly increased sensitivity to glare, particularly from oncoming headlights while driving at night, which culminated tonight in a blinding sensation from the hallway light. The patient reports seeing halos around bright lights and has noticed a faded colour perception with an overall yellowish tint to vision over the past few months. There has been a frequent need for prescription changes in corrective eyewear without relief. Tonight, difficulty reading fine print in low light environments and decreased contrast sensitivity made steps and curbs impossible to distinguish, resulting in a near-miss fall down a flight of three internal stairs. The patient denies double vision or monocular diplopia.
Functional Status: Previously independent; currently confined to the armchair due to acute fear of mobilizing.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls; lives in a split-level home with poor ambient lighting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Appears shaken and highly anxious, sitting rigidly in an armchair in a dimly lit room.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal and neurological exams reveal no signs of blunt force trauma, focal neurological deficits, or fractures from the near-miss fall. Cranial nerve examination is grossly intact. Ophthalmological evaluation using a penlight reveals a visible cloudy and opaque appearance of the lens bilaterally. Confrontation testing demonstrates gradual painless blurred vision. When tested with a direct light source, the patient exhibits increased glare sensitivity/halos and squints aggressively. The patient accurately reports a yellowish tint to vision when viewing a white clinical document.
Environmental Observations: Hallway lighting is extremely harsh, creating significant glare on uncarpeted wooden stairs. Contrast between stair treads is non-existent.
Medication Reconciliation: No specific visual side effects expected from current antihypertensive medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced Cataracts with acute environmental safety compromise - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of ocular pain, headache, nausea, vomiting, or conjunctival injection.

Macular Degeneration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of central scotoma or distortion of straight lines on Amsler grid testing; visual loss is global and glare-predominant.

Transient Ischemic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of sudden, curtain-like monocular vision loss (amaurosis fugax) or accompanying motor/sensory deficits; vision changes are chronic and bilateral.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a high-risk near-miss fall precipitated by severe functional visual impairment, specifically extreme glare sensitivity and loss of contrast. Telephone triage identified the acute risk of recurrent, potentially catastrophic traumatic injury if the patient was left unassessed overnight. Delaying care until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in an unsafe environment without a medical evaluation to exclude occult injury from the stumble, or practical strategies to mobilize safely. This urgent visit allowed the clinician to rule out acute ocular or neurological emergencies, modify the immediate lighting environment to reduce glare, and establish a safe nighttime mobility plan to prevent a hip fracture or head injury.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassured the patient regarding the absence of acute traumatic injury. Recommended comprehensive optometry and ophthalmology review. Directed the patient to strictly cease driving immediately.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured environmental adaptation and mobility routine. Step 1: The patient pauses at the top of any steps, turns on soft indirect lighting rather than harsh overhead lights, and verbalizes "hand on rail, foot on edge" before descending. Step 2: Upon successful safe navigation of the stairs using this method, the patient rewards themselves with a scheduled phone call to a family member or engaging in a preferred hobby. Cause-and-effect: Consciously slowing down and utilizing a verbal safety checklist, followed by a positive social reward, reinforces safe mobility habits and directly overrides the panic response, reducing fall risk.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an occupational therapist for home safety and contrast modification.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgently recommended the installation of high-contrast tape on the edges of all stairs and anti-glare nightlights.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within one week to coordinate urgent ophthalmology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if there is sudden, complete loss of vision, severe eye pain, new onset of flashing lights or floaters, or if a physical fall occurs resulting in head trauma or inability to bear weight.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical exam strongly suggests dense cataracts, but definitive staging requires formal slit-lamp examination by an eye specialist.
Clinical Course: Cataracts are progressive; the vision will not naturally improve and will continue to degrade contrast sensitivity until surgically corrected.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department immediately if you experience a sudden total loss of vision, intense physical pain in your eye, or if you fall and hit your head. You must see your GP next week to get the surgical referral process started.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lens inside your eye has become cloudy, like a frosted window. This scatters incoming light, causing the severe glare and washing out the contrast so you cannot see the edge of the stairs. Surgery is the only way to replace the cloudy lens.
Actionable Steps: Do not drive under any circumstances. Use soft, indirect lighting to minimize glare. Pause and physically hold the handrail before using stairs, and reward your deliberate safety routine by doing an activity you enjoy immediately afterward.

